  "title": "Conditional Rendering in JSX",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Build your First Web App with React"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Conditional Rendering in JSX"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can use JavaScript logic inside JSX with {}. Thus, you can use an if or a ternary, or a short-circuit like condition && <Something />."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here are some examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "1. Ternary Expression"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "{entries.length === 0 ? <p>No entries yet.</p> : <List />}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "2. Short-Circuit"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can render the list separately after running this code:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "{entries.length === 0 && <p>No entries yet.</p>}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "3. Code Before the return Statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can compute the content in a variable before using return, and then use the variable in your JSX."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Next, we'll learn how to display a message when the list is empty."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
